        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting the shake but not the caffeine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 200 mg thermogenic shake plus two coffees and a pre-workout can put you well past the FDA's 400 mg daily guide. Treat the caffeine in your protein as part of your daily total, skip other caffeine on days you use it, and never take a thermogenic protein after mid-afternoon if sleep matters to you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stacking it with a capsule fat burner</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many people use a thermogenic protein alongside a fat burner capsule and a pre-workout, which often triples up on caffeine and can multiply green tea extract. The small benefits do not add up neatly, but the side effects and the liver risk from concentrated extracts can. Pick one source of stimulant, if any, and read every label you are taking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Believing the before-and-after numbers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Headline claims such as pounds lost in 60 days almost always come from a study of one ingredient, usually alongside a low-calorie diet, not from the protein powder you are buying. The diet did most of the work. Use protein per calorie and cost per gram to choose, and treat weight-loss figures on the tub as marketing, not a forecast.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using two scoops when one was the plan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Thermogenic powders often define a serving as two scoops, and the caffeine, calories and price per serving are all doubled compared with what people expect. Read the serving size on the panel, decide how many scoops you will really use, and recalculate protein per 100 calories and caffeine per shake on that basis before you compare products.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the protein quality underneath</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some fat-loss powders use blends that give fewer grams of protein per calorie than a plain isolate. Since protein keeps you full and protects muscle in a deficit, a weaker base is a real cost. If a ripped formula delivers under about 20 g of protein per 100 calories, a lean isolate is the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do fat burner protein powders actually work?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Only a little beyond what the protein itself does. Caffeine has the most consistent evidence, with small effects on energy use and body fat, and CLA and L-carnitine show modest results in some trials. Green coffee and green tea extracts have weak or inconsistent evidence. In a calorie deficit with enough protein, the add-ins might help at the margin; without a deficit, they will not produce fat loss.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is a thermogenic protein better than a regular whey isolate for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Usually not. A lean isolate gives the same or more protein per calorie, often costs the same or less per gram, contains no stimulants and can be used at any time of day. A thermogenic protein mainly adds convenience if you want caffeine with your morning shake. You can get the same effect by mixing a plain unflavored isolate into coffee.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much caffeine is in a fat burner protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It varies widely. GNC's current Wheybolic Ripped is reported at 200 mg per two-scoop serving, roughly two cups of coffee. MuscleTech Nitro-Tech Ripped uses green coffee extract and did not print a caffeine total on the label we saw. Always read the panel for the exact flavor and tub you buy, and count it toward the FDA's 400 mg daily guide for healthy adults.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is green tea extract in protein powder safe?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  At modest doses it is generally well tolerated, but concentrated green tea extract has been linked to rare cases of liver injury, especially at high doses and on an empty stomach. EFSA flagged liver enzyme rises at 800 mg or more of EGCG a day from supplements. Avoid it if you have liver concerns, and do not stack several products that contain it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I drink a thermogenic protein shake at night?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  We would not. Most contain caffeine or other stimulants, and caffeine's effects can last many hours, so an evening shake can disrupt your sleep, which in turn makes appetite harder to manage. For an evening or bedtime shake, use a plain whey isolate or a casein protein without stimulants, and keep any thermogenic shake to the morning or before training.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Who should avoid fat burner protein powders?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Anyone who is pregnant or breastfeeding, under 18, sensitive to caffeine, or living with high blood pressure, heart rhythm problems, anxiety, thyroid conditions or liver disease should avoid them or speak to a doctor first. The same applies if you take regular medication, including stimulants, antidepressants or blood thinners. This guide is general information and not medical advice.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What should I look for if I still want one?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Look for at least 20 g of protein per 100 calories, every add-in listed with its own dose rather than a proprietary blend, a clearly printed caffeine total, and ideally a third-party certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. Compare cost per 25 g of protein against a plain isolate, and only pay a premium if the convenience is worth it to you.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

